   $q$Triangles $ABC$ and $DEF$ have right angles at $C$ and $F$. The hypotenuses are $AB = DE = 13$, and the legs $AC = DF = 5$. Using only these given parts, with no calculation, which criterion proves the triangles congruent?$q$, $q$[{"text":"HL","is_correct":true},{"text":"None; this is SSA, which never works","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-ssa-never-works-for-right-triangles"},{"text":"SAS","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"treated-non-included-angle-as-included"},{"text":"ASA","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"counted-sides-as-angles"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$HL$q$, $q$The given parts are a hypotenuse and a leg of two right triangles, which is exactly HL. The right angle is not between $AB$ and $AC$, so SAS does not fit. SSA fails in general, but when the angle is a right angle the triangle is fixed, which is why HL is a valid criterion.$q$, 'draft', null)
