   $q$In triangles $ABC$ and $DEF$, it is given that $AB = DE$, $BC = EF$, and $\angle A = \angle D$. Neither triangle is a right triangle. Which statement is correct?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Congruence is not proved; the angle is not between the two sides, and SSA can fit two different triangles","is_correct":true},{"text":"The triangles are congruent by SAS","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"treated-non-included-angle-as-included"},{"text":"The triangles are congruent by HL","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-right-triangle-rule-without-right-angle"},{"text":"The triangles are congruent by SSS","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"counted-angle-as-a-side"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Congruence is not proved; the angle is not between the two sides, and SSA can fit two different triangles$q$, $q$$\angle A$ is formed by sides $AB$ and $AC$, so it is not between $AB$ and $BC$. Two sides and a non-included angle (SSA) can sometimes be built into two different triangles, so this information does not prove congruence. SAS needs the angle between the two given sides.$q$, 'draft', null)
