   $q$Triangle $PQR$ has angles of $40^\circ$, $60^\circ$, and $80^\circ$. Triangle $XYZ$ has the same three angle measures, but each of its sides is twice as long as the matching side of triangle $PQR$. What does this example show?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Such a triangle $XYZ$ cannot exist, because the angles fix the side lengths","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-angles-determine-side-lengths"},{"text":"The triangles are congruent by AAA","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"believed-aaa-proves-congruence"},{"text":"The triangles are congruent because a dilation is a rigid motion","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-dilation-is-rigid"},{"text":"Matching all three angles does not prove congruence, because the triangles can differ in size","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Matching all three angles does not prove congruence, because the triangles can differ in size$q$, $q$The two triangles have the same shape but different sizes, so they are not congruent even though all three pairs of angles match. That is why AAA is not a congruence criterion. A dilation changes lengths, so it is not a rigid motion.$q$, 'draft', null)
