   $q$In triangles $ABC$ and $DEF$, it is given that $\angle A = \angle D$ and $AB = DE$. Which additional fact would NOT be enough to prove the triangles congruent?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$BC = EF$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$\\angle C = \\angle F$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"rejected-valid-aas"},{"text":"$\\angle B = \\angle E$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"rejected-valid-asa"},{"text":"$AC = DF$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"rejected-valid-sas"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$BC = EF$$q$, $q$Adding $\angle B = \angle E$ gives ASA, since $AB$ lies between angles $A$ and $B$. Adding $AC = DF$ gives SAS, since $\angle A$ lies between $AB$ and $AC$. Adding $\angle C = \angle F$ gives AAS. Adding $BC = EF$ gives two sides and an angle that is not between them (SSA), which does not prove congruence.$q$, 'draft', null)
