   $q$Quadrilateral $ABCD$ is a kite with $AB = AD$ and $CB = CD$. Diagonal $AC$ splits it into triangles $ABC$ and $ADC$. No angle measures are given. Which criterion proves triangle $ABC$ congruent to triangle $ADC$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"HL","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-diagonal-makes-right-angles"},{"text":"SSS","is_correct":true},{"text":"Not enough information","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-shared-side"},{"text":"SAS","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-an-angle-was-given"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$SSS$q$, $q$Two pairs of sides are given: $AB = AD$ and $CB = CD$. The diagonal $AC$ is a side of both triangles, so $AC = AC$ by the reflexive property. Three pairs of congruent sides is SSS. No angle or right angle is given, so SAS and HL do not apply.$q$, 'draft', null)
