   $q$Segments $AE$ and $BD$ cross at point $C$. It is given that $AC = EC$ and $\angle A = \angle E$. Also, $\angle ACB = \angle ECD$ because they are vertical angles. In triangles $ABC$ and $EDC$, side $AC$ lies between the angle at $A$ and the angle at $C$. Which criterion proves the triangles congruent?$q$, $q$[{"text":"SAS","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"counted-angles-as-sides"},{"text":"HL","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-right-triangle-rule-without-right-angle"},{"text":"SSS","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-all-sides-given"},{"text":"ASA","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$ASA$q$, $q$The congruent parts are $\angle A = \angle E$, side $AC = EC$, and the vertical angles $\angle ACB = \angle ECD$. The side lies between the two angles, so the criterion is ASA. Only one pair of sides is known, so SAS and SSS cannot be used.$q$, 'draft', null)
