   $q$Lena tries to build triangle $ABC$ with $\angle A = 30^\circ$, $AB = 10$, and $BC = 6$. The distance from $B$ to the line through $A$ and $C$ is $10 \cdot \sin 30^\circ = 5$. How many non-congruent triangles fit these measurements?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Infinitely many","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-fixed-side-lengths"},{"text":"$1$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-ssa-determines-a-triangle"},{"text":"$0$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-side-too-short-to-reach"},{"text":"$2$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$2$$q$, $q$Side $BC = 6$ swings from $B$ and must land on the line through $A$ and $C$. Since $6$ is longer than the distance $5$ but shorter than $AB = 10$, it meets that line at two points on the same side of $A$, giving two different triangles. This is why SSA does not prove congruence.$q$, 'draft', null)
