   $q$Triangles $ABC$ and $DEF$ have right angles at $C$ and $F$. In triangle $ABC$, hypotenuse $AB = 13$ and leg $AC = 5$. In triangle $DEF$, hypotenuse $DE = 2x + 3$ and leg $DF = 5$. For what value of $x$ can the triangles be proved congruent by HL?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$x = 5$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$x = 1$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"matched-hypotenuse-to-leg"},{"text":"$x = 8$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"added-instead-of-subtracted-when-solving"},{"text":"$x = 4.5$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"matched-hypotenuse-to-other-leg"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$x = 5$$q$, $q$HL needs the hypotenuses equal and one pair of legs equal. The legs already match, $AC = DF = 5$, so set $2x + 3 = 13$. Then $2x = 10$ and $x = 5$. The value $x = 1$ sets the hypotenuse equal to the leg length $5$, which matches the wrong parts.$q$, 'draft', null)
