  ('c1bc804c-14bc-525b-9a42-23d3f8deef22', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'triangle-congruence'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Triangle $ABC$ has vertices $A(0, 0)$, $B(3, 0)$, and $C(0, 4)$. Triangle $DEF$ has vertices $D(5, 1)$, $E(5, 4)$, and $F(1, 1)$. Which sequence of rigid motions maps $A$ to $D$, $B$ to $E$, and $C$ to $F$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Translate 5 units right and 1 unit up only","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-change-in-orientation"},{"text":"Reflect across the $y$-axis, then translate 5 units right and 1 unit up","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-reflection-instead-of-rotation"},{"text":"Rotate $90^\\circ$ clockwise about the origin, then translate 5 units right and 1 unit up","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"rotated-in-wrong-direction"},{"text":"Rotate $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise about the origin, then translate 5 units right and 1 unit up","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Rotate $90^\circ$ counterclockwise about the origin, then translate 5 units right and 1 unit up$q$, $q$A $90^\circ$ counterclockwise rotation about the origin sends $(x, y)$ to $(-y, x)$: $A(0, 0)$ stays at $(0, 0)$, $B(3, 0)$ goes to $(0, 3)$, and $C(0, 4)$ goes to $(-4, 0)$. Translating 5 right and 1 up gives $(5, 1)$, $(5, 4)$, and $(1, 1)$, which are $D$, $E$, and $F$. A clockwise turn would send $B$ to $(0, -3)$ and then to $(5, -2)$, not $E$.$q$, 'draft', null)
